\f1\fs22 Jamison Hardy spent the afternoon deciding who to consult.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Scientists would say yes. Doctors would say yes, and would say it publicly, in a manner that would make anyone who disagreed sound like a murderer. Government ministers would leak, from the ordinary human need to be the first at a dinner party to know something.\
Hardy wanted an answer that would stand up, from people with no stake in the outcome, and no standing to be quoted.\
So he looked at his finance people, and from the finance department he looked at the juniors. There were accountants, economists, finance advisers and a woman from Treasury Modelling whose job title ran to fourteen words and described, in essence, the decorating of graphs. They were people who spent their days building models nobody read, of futures nobody intended to have, for reports that existed so that a decision already taken could be described afterwards as evidence-based. They were good at it. They were good at it the way a well-made hinge is good: silent, frictionless, and without any expectation of being looked at.\
He had a dozen of them collected in the dark of night, and not told where they were going.\
They were going to the office tower in the Pale, though nobody called it that back then; it was simply the part of the inner city where Hardy had labs. The lift went down for a long time. It went down long enough that two of the juniors got the giggles, and then stopped, as people do when a lift keeps going without a light on the panel changing.\
The corridor went on for a long time too; at a cross-roads, Hardy stopped and read a sign. He stood in front of it in a suit worth more than the annual maintenance budget of the level he was standing on, in a building he owned outright. He read the sign twice then went left.\
Hardy led them to a conference room. The lights wheezed on and shone like they were still asleep. In the corner shadows stood a whiteboard like a piece of unwanted furniture. It had not been properly cleaned since someone had erased Cook\'92s plan to find the Northwest Passage. There was a jug of water on a table and twelve chairs. The juniors sat; Hardy stood.\
\'93I don\'92t know any of your names,\'94 he said, \'93and I have declined to be told them. Please don\'92t tell me. You have been brought here precisely because I don\'92t know you.\'94\
They took this as a security formality; half of them felt important.\
Hardy gave them the question. What happens to the economy if disease ends? Not slows, not becomes manageable: 
\f2\i ends
\f1\i0 \'97everyone lives to their full expected term. He gave them a tablet that had nothing but a chatbot on it; it would answer any question they cared to put to it\'97which impressed them\'97and would prevent any of them from straying beyond the room, the bathrooms and the corridor between\'97which confused and scared and impressed them. He gave them seven hours.\
One of them asked why seven hours, in the reasonable manner of a person hoping to be granted eight, and Hardy said, \'93Operational.\'94 The junior asked the chatbot and the chatbot said, \'93Operational\'94.\
\'93Do you want demo or a slide deck?\'94\
\'93Is there a template?\'94\
\'93Are we assuming current tax settings, or should we model reform?\'94\
\'93Will there be doughnuts?\'94\
One of the juniors wanted to put their work-product under version control.\
\'93Yes, there will be doughnuts,\'94 was the only answer Hardy offered. There would be catering and it would be good. It was a very good one. The coffee would be from the executive floor, and he had checked.\
They were delighted. Several of them said so.\
He wished them well and left them to it.
